  "confidence": 0.78,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["J4", "U2", "U3"],
      "pins": ["J4.1", "J4.4", "U2.5", "U2.6", "U3.5", "U3.6"],
      "nets": ["PWM", "SDA"],
      "observed": "On the AVR-ISP-6 header J4, pin 1 (MISO) carries the net label 'PWM' and pin 4 (MOSI) carries the net label 'SDA'. On the ATtiny microcontrollers, MISO is PB0 which is on the 'SDA' net, and MOSI is PB1 which is on the 'PWM' net. The MISO and MOSI connections to the ISP header are therefore swapped.",
      "expected": "J4 pin 1 (MISO) should be on the SDA net (PB0) and J4 pin 4 (MOSI) should be on the PWM net (PB1), matching the MCU's ISP pin-to-net assignments so that an external programmer can communicate with the chip.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "J4 pin 1 name 'MISO' with box label 'PWM'; J4 pin 4 name 'MOSI' with box label 'SDA' (sheet /, page 1)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U2 pin 5 (AREF/PB0) box label 'SDA'; U2 pin 6 (PB1) box label 'PWM' (sheet /, page 2 left tile)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "an ATtiny-driven fan controller"}
      ],
      "impact": "ISP programming and debugging via J4 would fail: the programmer's MISO line drives the MCU's MOSI pin and the programmer's MOSI line reads the MCU's MISO pin, so the SPI handshake required for programming cannot complete. The board could not be flashed through the header.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    },
    {
      "category": "footprint_mismatch",
      "components": ["U1"],
      "pins": ["U1.2", "U1.3"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "VCC"],
      "observed": "U1 (LM117-5.0) pin 2 is named 'VI' and connected to +5V (the input rail), while pin 3 is named 'VO' and connected to VCC (the regulated output). In the standard TO-220 LM117 package, pad 2 = VOUT and pad 3 = VIN, so the schematic's input and output are swapped relative to the physical footprint pads.",
      "expected": "Pad 2 (VOUT per datasheet) should connect to VCC (regulated output) and pad 3 (VIN per datasheet) should connect to +5V (input). The symbol pin numbering must match the footprint pad numbering.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U1 pin 2 'VI' → +5V with C2 100nF decoupling; U1 pin 3 'VO' → VCC with C1 100nF decoupling; U1 pin 1 'GND' → GND (sheet /, page 2 left tile)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "an ATtiny-driven fan controller"}
      ],
      "impact": "If the standard TO-220 footprint is used, the regulator is wired backwards: the input voltage is applied to the output pad and the regulated output is taken from the input pad. The LM117 would not regulate, VCC would be undefined or near zero, and the fan power path (Q1) and TACH pull-up (R6) would fail.",
      "confidence": 0.65
    },
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["J3", "U2", "U3"],
      "pins": ["J3.2", "U2.2", "U3.3"],
      "nets": ["PD1", "POT"],
      "observed": "The potentiometer connector J3 pin 2 (wiper) carries net label 'PD1', while the MCU pins that should read the potentiometer — U2 pin 2 (PB3) and U3 pin 3 (PB3) — carry net label 'POT'. Because the two label strings differ, no net connects the potentiometer wiper to the MCU ADC input.",
      "expected": "The potentiometer wiper and the MCU PB3 pin should share the same net label so the speed-setting potentiometer is read by the microcontroller.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "J3 pin 2 box label 'PD1' (sheet /, page 1)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U2 pin 2 box label 'POT'; U3 pin 3 box label 'POT' (sheet /, page 2 left tile)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The fan-speed potentiometer would be electrically disconnected from the MCU. The fan controller would not respond to the user's speed setting.",
      "confidence": 0.50
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The ISP header J4 has its MISO and MOSI pins connected to the wrong nets (swapped relative to the ATtiny's PB0/PB1 assignments), which would prevent programming. The LM117 regulator U1 appears to have its input and output connected to the wrong footprint pads, and the potentiometer net label on J3 ('PD1') does not match the MCU pin label ('POT'), leaving the speed control disconnected."
}

